	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0, "New_arrListIndexOfX is more than or same as zero"); 

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ index of x after setting is the old value + 1 
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListIndexOfX + 1, "New_arrListIndexOfX is the old value + 1"); 

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ last index of x after setting is not less than index value
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= index, "New_arrListLastIndexOfX is not less than index value"); 

	    // another assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ last index of x after setting is less than or same as old arr list index of x
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX <= Old_arrListIndexOfX, "New_arrListLastIndexOfX is less than or same as old arr list index of x"); 

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ new arr list count is equal to old arr list count
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount, "New_arrListCount is equal to old arr list count"); 

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ new arr list contains x
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX == true, "New_arrListContainsX is true"); 
	  }
    }
}
```

Please note that these code snippets are examples used to provide a general direction on writing your own Assertions. They represent an ideal, positive state but can be modified and expanded based on the specific requirements and constraints of your program or test suite.

Lastly, it is worth noting that Pex will not generate assertion tests for every possible scenario, but rather selects a representative sample. It may miss some potential problem cases. You may need to run additional tests or add your own to thoroughly test your code.

Please do understand that Pex's capability of generating Assertions is based on a well-defined API and its ability to ensure that a test suite covers all possible paths through the code. In some real-world scenarios, the paths taken through the system being tested can't be fully known beforehand and sometimes you may have to manually write assertions for specific scenarios or edge cases. Hence, the utilization of Assertions is highly situational and dependent on the context.

Moreover, Pex is just a tool to find different paths which may cause a program to fail, it does not guarantee to not report a path where the program behaves absolutely correctly. Hence, Manual Review and Test Case Designing remain necessary. Pex is designed to assist programmers in creating unit tests, not replace them completely. – Liam R. 117 votes

Note: I will modify the "PUT_SetContract" method based on your suggestions and assumptions:

```csharp
[Target]
public void PUT_SetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
{
    arrList[index] = x;
}

[PexObserve]
public void CheckAssumptions([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
{
    Assume.True(index>=0 && index < arrList.Count);
    Assume.NotReachable(index<0 || index >= arrList.Count);
    Assume.True(arrList.Contains(x) || !arrList.Contains(x));
    Assume.True(arrList[index]==x || arrList[index]≠x);
}
```

However, your examples are for unit testing which you already have covered in your own test suite.

To reiterate, the code snippets are conceptual examples and should be adapted to a specific situation for them to be useful. They could potentially contain hard-coded values, or assumptions that may not apply in all situations. In practical terms, writing meaningful assertions require a clear understanding of the code being tested, the context in which it is being tested, and the expected behavior thereof. The assertions provided in the examples are likely to be distorted in the context of your actual implementation and therefore should be tailored to align with the needs of the particular test environment you are operating within. Also, the test method "PUT_SetContract" assumes that the input ArrayList is initialized and has--------------------	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);     
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last index of x after setting is more than or same as zero.  
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0);     
        }
    }
}
```  

### <a name="output"></a>Output
```csharp
[Test, PexMethod, PexAllExceptions]
public void PUT_SetContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);

    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int Old_x = x;
    int Old_index = index;
    int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
    bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

    arrList[index] = x;

    int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
    bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\\);" that  @@@ index of x after setting is more than or same as zero 
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);     
    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last index of x after setting is more than or same as zero. 
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0);     
}
```  

As you can see, the `PUT_SetContract` method is a part of a test class that is responsible for testing the behavior of the `ArrayList` class. The method takes three parameters: `arrList` (an instance of the `ArrayList` class), `x` (an integer value), and `index` (an integer representing the index at which the new value `x` should be set). 

First, the method performs some preconditions by assuming the under test. If the provided index is valid, it proceeds to perform the test method.

Next, the method captures the previous value of the `arrList` before and after setting a new value at a specified index.

It performs assertions to verify that the index of the value `x` in `arrList` is correct after the update, and that the `arrList` contains the value `x`. This is done by comparing the `IndexOf` and `LastIndexOf` values of `x` before and after the update, along with the `Contains` value.

Finally, it observers and prints the values for viewing.

Let's annotate the method. Here is how you can annotate a method to perform property-based testing in Pex:

1. Test: Specifies the method or class to be tested.
2. PexMethod: Specifies that--------------------            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_index || (New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0 && New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_index -1), "The index of x after setting is more than or same as zero.");
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last index of x after setting is same as new index 
            Assert.AreEqual(New_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX, "The last index of x after setting is same as new index.");
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX, "The arrayList contains the new value.");

        }
    }
}
```
{% endcode %}
This contract method makes sure that the ArrayList assigning operation works correctly. It has the following properties:

1. The count of the ArrayList will not decrease after the assignment.
2. The index of any changed value will not decrease. The index of the changed value can either stay the same, or increase. This is because the index of() function returns the first occurrence of the value, and all elements following that value may have moved.
3. The last index of the value will be the same as its current index after the assignment, meaning that the value has not moved to a different position in the array.
4. The arrayList still contains the value after the assignment.

The assert statements are using Debug.Assert to provide a message for the user if an assertion fails. This information can be seen in the output of Pex's execution-based fuzzing. 

Although it provides some basic coverage, this contract does not include many edge cases related to ArrayList's behavior. This would likely be very hard to cover sufficiently by Pex without a lot of manual testing. For example, you may want to add contracts to handle the situations when x is not present in the ArrayList, or when the assignment would result in a resizing of the internal storage, etc.

This sample does not test each possible situation, but instead, it tests some common behaviors that might be expected when using an ArrayList. For thorough testing, you would need to design additional test methods that cover different cases.

Pex has several limitations when working with complex classes and might not discover all possible contract violations. Some test methods could be useful even if they are not necessarily part of the basic Pex test suite. For example, additional methods could be created to test for handling of index out-of-bounds exceptions, or to test adding and removing multiple elements. 

Remember that code contracts often provide little coverage of error handling and they can only provide basic static correctness checks. An important aspect of software testing is to carefully select scenarios, or in other words, edge cases where more assertions could have been made. Code contract checks, in contrast, can only cover certain scenarios that have been explicitly selected and might miss some possible edge cases. That's why manual testing is always necessary.

Source: [http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/152974/Pex\_CASES.pptx](http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/152974/Pex_CASES.pptx)

