title: C++ Lambda Expressions (C++11 onwards)
date: 2015-09-07T20:48:48+00:00
author: Abhi
layout: post
categories: C++
---
In programming, a lambda expression or lambda function is a method of defining an anonymous function (or lambda function). Lambda functions are essentially syntactic sugar for one-line functions that can be defined without a name, and the compiler generates an unnamed, anonymous class under the hood.

Lambda functions are implemented in C++ using the concept of closures. A lambda function contains an unnamed function and variables from its surrounding scope that are accessible within the function. They allow us to define function objects in a very concise manner, and they are widely used when the need of a function object is temporary. Lambda functions are available in C++11 and later.

A basic syntax to define a lambda can be given as:

```cpp
[ captures ] ( parameters ) mutable(optional) exception-specification 
{  
   definition  
};  
```
Lambda functions can capture variables from the enclosing scope by value or by reference. They can also carry out updates to the captured values by using the "mutable" keyword.

Example:
  
```cpp
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
	// Lambda function to add two numbers
	auto add = [](int a, int b) { return a + b; };
	cout << "Sum: " << add(10, 5) << endl;

	return 0;
}
```
In the above example, a lambda function is defined to add two numbers. lambda functions can be used wherever function objects are required.

Note: Lambda functions can also make use of the "auto" keyword for type inference.

Lambda functions, closures and lambdas are powerful features of modern C++, enabling functional programming techniques to be applied more easily. They are used in many advanced C++ algorithms, containers (like vector, list etc), STL (Standard Template Library), etc. However, excessive use of lambda functions can cause more complex code that is hard to debug and maintain, so it's a skill that programmers should learn to control.
  
References:
  
C++ Lambda function - https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/lambda 

Understanding C++ Lambda Closures - https://www.bfilipek.com/2018/07/understanding-c-lambda-closures.html

Lambda functions in C++ are a bit unusual - http://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2014/n3878.pdf

C++ Lambda functions by Examples - https://thegeekybiz.com/c-lambda-functions/
  
Lambda in Modern C++ by Herb Sutter - https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fSGU4ZLsDIQ

Lambda Expressions in C++11,C++14 by Bartosz Milewski's Blog - https://bartoszmilewski.com/2014/12/10/c11-and-lambda-calculus/

A quick guide lambda (C++11) - https://www.bfilipek.com/2014/08/quick-guide-lambda-c11.html

Lambda Functions and Their Use Cases - https://www.ibm.com/blogs/dvr-podcast/2012/09/lambda-functions-and-their-use-cases/

Learnings from Google's C++ style guide for lambda functions - https://developers.google.com/style/lambda
  
Lambda Functions are a New Feature - https://www.fluentcpp.com/2018/--------------------            Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_index);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that @@@ Last index of x after setting is more than or same as zero 
            Debug.Assert(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= Old_index);

        }
    }
}
