## Incorporating Contract Tests and Static Checks into Automated Builds<a name="Incorporating_Contract_Tests_and_Static_Checks_into_Automated_Builds"></a>

To incorporate Contract Tests and Static Checking into automated builds, you can use several approaches depending on the tools and CI/CD infrastructure being used.

- **Use an Integrated Development Environment (IDE) for writing Contract Tests**
  
  Most IDEs like Visual Studio, Eclipse, or Visual Studio Code allow you to write unit tests directly in the editor, which can easily be integrated with automated builds.

- **Use Automated Test Runner for Contract Tests**
  
  Many automated test runners, like Junit for Java, NUnit for C#, or pytest for Python, can execute your Contract Tests automatically when you run your automated builds. You can configure these tools to run your tests as part of the build process, and fail the build when your Contract Tests fail.

- **Leverage Static Analysis tools**

  Tools like SonarQube or Crucible can perform static code analysis and spot potential issues before your code is merged into the mainline repository. They can spot potential bugs, security vulnerabilities, code smells, and more. The results generated by these tools can be integrated into the build process, failing the build when issues are found.

- **CI/CD pipeline for Contract Tests and Static Checking**

  Many CI/CD tools like Jenkins, GitLab CI/CD, or Azure DevOps offer pipeline orchestration capabilities, and integrate automated building, testing, and deploying processes. You can configure these pipelines to automatically run your Contract Tests and static checks as part of your automated builds or even as part of every commit or PR check. Failing these checks can block the completion of the pipeline or even trigger notifications for the associated team members.

These approaches showcase the concept of ensuring reliability, quality, and robustness in your software development process by incorporating automated tests, static code analysis, and builds. Remember to tailor your approach based on the specific requirements of your project and the CI/CD infrastructure in use.

