In the previous code, the PUT\_SetContract method, is a partial method of an arraylist contract test defined in 'ArrayListContractTest.cs'. It is defined to set a contract for putting a given value at a certain index of the ArrayList and tests the arraylist class invariants to ensure that they hold correct values even after the setting operation.

A list of assumptions are made such as: 
- 'index' must be a non-negative value and less than the count of items in the arraylist.
- The 'arrList' should not be null.

Before performing any setting operation, the old indexes, count of items, and contains of arraylist are recorded. After setting operation, the new indexes, count of items, and contains of arraylist are also recorded. All these recorded values are asserted to help in further validation.

This method demonstrates the implementation of an arraylist setting operation with its corresponding invariant checks done. 'ArrayListContractTest.ContractTestCase\*' is a placeholder for actual test names. These might not be executed due to the complexity of the contract testing and it's likely to be a slower test than a simple unit testing, but the purpose here is to check the functionality of the ArrayList under different circumstances.

An example of the output generated might be:
- Old ArrayList Count: 5
- New ArrayList Count: 5
- Old ArrayList x: 10
- Old ArrayList index: 3
- Old ArrayList IndexOf x: 3
- New ArrayList IndexOf x: 10 (the element at index 3 was replaced by 10)
- Old ArrayList LastIndexOf x: 3 
- New ArrayList LastIndexOf x: 4 (the element 10 moved from index 3 to 4)
- Old ArrayList contains x: True
- New ArrayList contains x: True

Please note again, this code is not to be run directly but to be included within the larger codebase to perform the testing operation of the ArrayList class.

This code can also be expanded with more test methods to include additional contracts for other ArrayList methods, for full coverage. However, the actual implementation and coverage will depend on the specific requirements and contract testing framework used.

This way, invariants of ArrayList are preserved while performing setting operations. If an invariant does not hold after the operation, the test method would fail, thereby, highlighting the issue causing the problem and would not be considered as a successful test.

In conclusion, Contract Testing provides a way to check an individual unit or an operational function against a set of conditions. In terms of testing an ArrayList, this methodology helps to verify the correct handling of a setting operation scenario and ensures that all the conditions of the ArrayList class are met. It is a valuable tool for developers as it saves time and resources, and helps debug and fix issues easier.

Keep in mind it's worth using some form of fuzzing, where random inputs are provided to test the code's resilience against unexpected or out of bound inputs. This will not only ensure code quality but can also reveal design flaws and bottlenecks.


The most common issues found in ArrayList Contract Testing are –
1. Incorrect Index Calculation.
2. Incorrect Value Comparison.
3. Inefficient Function Execution.
4. Failing to handle Edge cases.
5. Not considering Exception Handling.

If the contract testing is done without considering the possible error scenarios it may fail to detect these issues.

It is recommended to always design the tests in such a way that, for any operation (insertion/update), all possible scenarios--------------------            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ index of x after setting is same as index 
            Assert.AreEqual(index, New_arrListIndexOfX);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ last index of x after setting is more than or same as zero 
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0);
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ x exist in the arrlist after setting 
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX == Old_arrListContainsX);

        }


        [PexMethod, PexExploration]
        public void PUT_SetContract_Test([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x, int index)
        {
            // Arrange
            PexAssume.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_index = index;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

                arrList[index] = x;

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_index", Old_index);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

	    // An assertion using "Assert.IsTrue\(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0\);" that @@@ index of x after setting is more than or same as zero 
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX >= 0);
	    // An assertion using "Assert.AreEqual\(index, New_arrListIndexOfX\);" that @@@ index of x after setting is same as index 
            Assert.AreEqual(index, New_arrListIndexOfX);
	    // An assertion using "Assert.IsTrue\(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0\);" that @@@ last index of x after setting is more than or same as zero 
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= 0);
	    // An assertion using "Assert.IsTrue\(New_arrListContainsX == Old_arrListContainsX\);" that @@@ x exist in the arrlist after setting 
            Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX == Old_arrListContainsX);

        }

    }
}

In the test methods defined, PexAssume.IsTrue is used to specify preconditions for the method under test (arrList[index] = x). This allows for the PexExploration to select test cases from different possible states of the world that satisfy these assumptions.
